@River_ember @CharlesFLehman @Brzczszczkwcz @besttrousers Outside of some batching optimizations that can cause some race condition floating point mismatches, and the temperature control to intentionally “weight the die,” LLM computation is deterministic on the weights and input. Given the same weights and input, you will get the same results when temperature = 0.

Training is likewise mathematically deterministic, but there’s so many steps in the gradient descent and RL process that it is unpredictable - this is really where the power comes from, in recognizing the statistical patterns at scale and getting them embedded into trillions of parameter weights.

As to your second point, I don’t think constraints generated by external forces change the result there. It’s a difference in form, not degree, for a conscious entity to assay their situation and make a choice, versus running a deterministic algorithm (no matter how complex).
